[Event "Advance, Fantasy, Exchange and Panov"]
[ChapterName "The Advance: Short Variation"]
[Orientation "black"]
[LeadIn "1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3pP3/3P4/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]

{ [%ts 13:45] After d5, White doesn't have to play Nc3. The Advance Variation is now about as common as the main line, and Stjepan says it gives White the biggest advantage if played properly: White counts on more space and more piece activity. }
3... Bf5 { [%ts 14:18] Black's most common move. It gives Black an improved French: the French's problem bishop gets outside the pawn chain on f5 before the e-pawn moves. }
( 3... c5 ) ( 3... e6 ) ( 3... a6 ) ( 3... Qc7 )
4. Nf3 { [%ts 14:35] The main move, the Short Variation. Nigel Short used it with great success from the 90s; before that White played a much more aggressive line (next chapter). }
4... e6 { [%ts 14:59] Black closes the structure. It's extremely solid and hard to penetrate, and Black has a clear plan: break with c5 and develop the knights to d7 and e7. }
( 4... a6 ) ( 4... Bg4 ) ( 4... Qd7 ) ( 4... h5 )
5. Be2
5... Nd7
( 5... Ne7 ) ( 5... a5 ) ( 5... c5 ) ( 5... Be7 ) ( 5... a6 ) ( 5... h6 ) ( 5... Bg6 )
6. O-O
6... Ne7
( 6... a5 ) ( 6... a6 ) ( 6... Nh6 ) ( 6... Bg6 ) ( 6... Be7 )
7. Nbd2
7... c5 { [%ts 15:28] Striking in the center. White has a lot of options here: c4, taking on c5, Qe1, Re1. This is the starting position of the Short Variation. }
( 7... h6 ) ( 7... Qc7 ) ( 7... Rc8 ) ( 7... Qb6 ) ( 7... Ng6 )
