[Event "Advance, Fantasy, Exchange and Panov"]
[ChapterName "The Advance: the old 4.Nc3 attack"]
[Orientation "black"]
[LeadIn "1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 Bf5 4.Nc3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "rn1qkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3pPb2/3P4/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 2 4"]

{ [%ts 15:48] The old way of meeting the Advance: Nc3. It gives White a lot of attacking chances, so Black has to be extra careful. }
4... e6 { [%ts 16:05] Played in 99% of games from here, before White pushes g4. }
( 4... h5 ) ( 4... a6 ) ( 4... Nd7 ) ( 4... h6 )
5. g4 { [%ts 16:05] White pushes on the kingside at once. The pawn on g4 is defended by the queen, so it can't be taken. }
5... Bg6
6. Nge2 { [%ts 16:13] Preparing Nf4 to challenge the bishop on g6. }
6... c5 { [%ts 16:21] Black has to strike in the center and seek counterplay immediately. If he doesn't, he'll just be smashed on the kingside without any chances. }
( 6... Nd7 ) ( 6... Be7 ) ( 6... h6 ) ( 6... Ne7 )
7. h4 { [%ts 16:29] Now h4 threatens to trap the bishop with h5, so Black has to react. }
7... h5 { [%ts 16:33] The best reaction: sacrifice a pawn on h5. }
( 7... h6 { [%ts 16:33] Stjepan finds h6 too passive, giving White a lot of play, though the engine rates it close. Play his h5. } )
8. Nf4
8... Bh7 { [%ts 16:45] Allowing Nxg6 fxg6 would be horrible, so the bishop retreats and Black gives up the h5 pawn. }
( 8... cxd4 ) ( 8... Nc6 )
9. Nxh5
9... cxd4
( 9... Nc6 ) ( 9... Qa5 ) ( 9... a6 )
10. Nb5 { [%ts 17:00] The knight heads for b5, eyeing c7 and the pawn on d4. }
10... Nc6 { [%ts 17:02] The starting position of this line. Black has given up a pawn, but in any late middlegame or endgame he'll be much better: his central pawns dominate half the board, and White's king is very unsafe. }
( 10... d3 ) ( 10... a6 )
