{ [%ts 19:15] The third most popular reply to d5 is f3, the Fantasy Variation (also called the Tartakower). White reinforces e4 with a pawn and asks Black to take, so he can recapture towards the center. }
3... dxe4
( 3... e6 { [%ts 20:37] The more passive way: e6 just solidifies. The downside is that White never has to take on d5, so the bishop on c8 is stuck like in the French. Here, take on e4. } ) ( 3... Qb6 ) ( 3... g6 ) ( 3... a6 )
4. fxe4
4... e5 { [%ts 19:48] The only active move: strike in the center and disrupt White's idea immediately. }
5. Nf3
5... Bg4 { [%ts 19:56] Pinning the knight. }
( 5... exd4 ) ( 5... Be6 ) ( 5... Nf6 )
6. Bc4
6... Nd7
7. c3 { [%ts 20:04] The starting position of the Fantasy main line. Engines think White is slightly better, but Stjepan likes Black: White's pawns are overextended, the diagonal towards his king is weak if he doesn't castle soon, and the center is easy to undermine until White is left with hanging pawns. }
*

