{ [%ts 21:26] The Exchange Variation: White trades the central pawns, simplifies, and argues that his extra tempo is enough for a favorable position. }
3... cxd5
4. c4 { [%ts 22:48] The Panov, or Panov-Botvinnik, Attack: one of the most aggressive ways to fight the Caro-Kann, and a favorite of strong players. White accepts an isolated queen's pawn, but in return Black has nothing left in the center and White gets a lot of piece activity. }
( 4. Bd3 { [%ts 21:51] Fischer's way: simply develop with Bd3, castle kingside, put the knight on d2 and play c3. Slightly passive, but it gives White chances. } 4... Nc6 ( 4... Nf6 ) ( 4... e6 ) ( 4... a6 ) ( 4... g6 ) 5. c3 5... Nf6 { [%ts 22:15] Black puts the knight on f6 and develops normally. } ( 5... e5 ) ( 5... Qc7 ) ( 5... g6 ) ( 5... e6 ) )
4... Nf6 { [%ts 23:12] The most common move: develop the knight to f6 and support d5. }
( 4... e6 ) ( 4... a6 ) ( 4... g6 ) ( 4... Nc6 )
5. Nc3
5... e6
( 5... Nc6 ) ( 5... g6 ) ( 5... a6 ) ( 5... dxc4 )
